    {
      "prompt": "Identify the species undergoing oxidation in this discharge reaction: \nZn(s) + CuSO4(aq) -> ZnSO4(aq) + Cu(s).\nExplain why Zinc loses electrons while Copper ions gain them, using relative electron affinity concepts.",
      "answer": "Zinc undergoes oxidation (loses 2e-). Zn has a lower ionization energy and more negative reduction potential than Cu. Electrons flow from the atom with greater tendency to lose charge toward the species needing those electrons.",
      "rubric": "- Correctly identifies Zinc as oxidized.\n- Explains direction via relative electron affinity or oxidation state change (+2 for Zn, +1 for Cu ion).",
      "skill": "explain"
    },
    {
      "prompt": "In the reaction 2Mg(s) + O2(g) -> 2MgO(s), determine which element is reduced. Draw a single half-reaction showing electron transfer direction and justify your choice based on oxidation state changes.",
      "answer": "Oxygen (0 to -2). Oxygen gains electrons from Mg to achieve octet stability, indicated by its decrease in formal charge number during bond formation with magnesium.",
      "rubric": "- Correctly identifies oxygen as the reduced species.\n- Shows accurate half-reaction logic (gain of 4e- per O atom) and links oxidation state drop (-2 vs 0).",
      "skill": "produce"
    },
    {
      "prompt": "A learner claims: 'Oxidation is gaining electrons because atoms want to be negative.' \nWrite the correct statement contrasting this misconception. Provide a real-world example where an element gains positive charge (oxidized) and explain why the error occurs.",
      "answer": "Corrected Statement: Oxidation is loss of electrons, leading to increased positivity. Example: Mg becomes +2 in salts. Error arises from confusing 'negative oxidation state' with physical electron count; students associate attraction for protons as gain rather than release.",
      "rubric": "- Correctly defines oxidation as electron loss and notes resulting positive charge.\n- Provides a clear example of an element increasing oxidation number while debunking the negative-electron myth.",
      "skill": "critique"
    }
  ],
