      "prompt": "Your basil turns yellow at the base while your tomato leaves show distinct purple veining on undersides. Which specific nutrient deficiency does this symptom pattern suggest for your mixed containers, and what single amendment type should you apply to correct it immediately?",
      "answer": "The symptoms indicate Nitrogen (N) deficiency in basil and Phosphorus (P) deficiency in tomatoes due to the distinct purple venation caused by sugar accumulation when P is low. Apply compost or well-rotted manure for N-rich growth, or bone meal/superphosphate for P specifically.",
      "rubric": "- Identifies both Nitrogen and Phosphorus deficiencies based on specific symptoms.\n- Suggests appropriate organic amendment types (compost/manure vs bone meal/superphosphate).",
      "skill": "apply"
    },
    {
      "prompt": "You are planning your spring planting schedule but do not have access to soil testing kits yet. Based on general urban gardening standards, create a step-by-step protocol for determining the current state of N-P-K levels in your potting mix before adding any amendments.",
      "answer": "1. Perform visual inspection for chlorosis or purpling.\n2. Assess leaf color intensity and plant growth vigor as proxies.\n3. Use a simple DTC (Dye Test) kit if available, or observe soil reaction with vinegar/bicarbonate pH checks to estimate nutrient availability contextually.",
      "rubric": "- Outlines visual indicators for N/P/K assessment without lab data.\n- Includes at least one low-cost diagnostic method suitable for urban settings.",
      "skill": "explain"
    },
    {
      "prompt": "A neighbor claims that adding extra nitrogen fertilizer alone will fix her fading tomato plants, regardless of other soil conditions. What is the flaw in this 'N-only' assumption based on the relationship between nutrients and plant health?",
      "answer": "The flaw assumes all yellowing is Nitrogen deficiency; it ignores Phosphorus (purple leaves) or Potassium (scorched tips/fruit issues). Adding N without balancing P/K can cause lockout effects where plants cannot access other essential elements, leading to poor growth despite higher nitrogen.",
      "rubric": "- Correctly identifies that yellowing may stem from P or K deficiency.\n- Explains the concept of nutrient imbalance or 'lockout' affecting plant health.",
      "skill": "critique"
    }
  ],
